{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1197 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "052769691d08062acddb8ce4747571fc3995e7970f17b741fdbb00dee49b19a4" :edb/db-before-t 1196 :edb/db-after-t 1197 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534509 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:23:06.094Z" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186149981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186149985 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186149981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423615 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186149981 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149986 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151943 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/81/81c99a47d72cc5e259acc8b4b33bf58c71c5d20e6c695beeeb71e0c784b614d5.png) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186151943 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/fb/fb428b64d8d1fc020f343aea11f50ad92158ad4dcbf611b5decb44bd79ac221c.svg) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151943 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/02/02a95bd3513fcd70ea74936f330472e5bf5a1e14b189da9c30c5876782d12632.png)" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186151943 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/7a/7abb144e511574809625c59d5ed6b7658983fc646bd0e6d6327f9f12356b8f08.svg)" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151944 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/b2/b22e2ffa6f6d88665e120e8dad82961e72f8f4a99484e8f81de311a66ab25988.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/28/28f79983a195fa0152832aebd334cd8e1aaaa977dd9016347a3152403fd390c7.svg)" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151944 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/f7/f7e27a29b580edc016bf479e983cb17c5e61ebb800b03e671b89b5d556056533.png) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cce279327716181e5b5c5c1e95e7eac34c2b93a78a1f7e773393ffbf608cb1f4.png)" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186151944 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/45/4588c7d7ae4fd6cbc749373d83ab2d81e6a228f0cac03d5b959f4177f3f4cdf9.svg)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/fa/fa2ad666a6b80eac26bad12db322fa361578d94327e230d7c520c950add78923.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151944 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/f0/f083a27cf3de0a6150020b6f5887096258880d4f5e30de1cfb3bb091b79a16f9.png)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/96/96f39576ea8196a88248035262eb7cd006c134717f695b23c575667b6562f6e0.png)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186151945 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/af/afbd6ce90f2192c311c38ced9c1d9512c48bbf9a5e06236415ef9fe70883f9be.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2ccba053acf8d050e305c3768c970ae635f4583163a867b6ef11f4e565ac1ae.svg)" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151945 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/f7/f7e27a29b580edc016bf479e983cb17c5e61ebb800b03e671b89b5d556056533.png) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/70/70f59cf06f15bb416d32ffa9b28d604f4a842273ac8bd04d9f59b121ca1dc40d.png)" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186151945 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/6b/6b4306172b78c1e59bf66c8c08bf864aac2bcab65d4b1cc08ba4ddab0e5f1da3.svg)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/b3/b3681d7f6c71d06ede32616ceec12ccd71da28373ee88eab451211af5a579212.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186151945 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/9c/9c6ae7fc8b0c044bf2286ea2b9d41c7a427deebf8184a3515ad2b20edc682afc.png)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/4a/4a0d70f028211fafeab4353e5c5e34cb1895be2350f5fd0211b6e76856dea3e0.png)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186152155 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423620 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186152157 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423621 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186152330 1050 780.0 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186152579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186152579 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A$. Determine if $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is a basis of $Col(A)$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $a_{3} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186152579 1071 "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186152579 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 3=-3.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186153352 1064 "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n![](images/6f/6f8184c584bac2ae9f4f0655d6de2f05bae48142caa729cc24faa30eb5e38774.svg)\n\nthe column space is\n\n![](images/83/83a4941812514b331349e96637ba4fc3d9686e85704506855f931efc3ed3d539.svg)\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186153352 1064 "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe column space is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186153353 1064 "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n![](images/c8/c8e10a79d647ed7867d42eae1eff7d37840f541cfce26e47d84f66da4e9f0c96.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n![](images/84/84cdf6af82bd0d301ca50b1c7c41bccbd0a3cfb42be17bc84ec2f12e46032161.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/22/22d4eb11af0168d4fabc8b3ebbe1befd24620db11b6a31709472ba91bb1ed794.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/f3/f3f4e0d48c58be10684eada3fd320001dc056f2d1070707d9443c9533b7b5e35.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/cf/cf66dc341420f6c399e1ad7e108986505cb349d3aba1aeaacee636e7b6ec4b94.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/84/847a4dd2e37e6e577bd9a38160f03bcb5839fbf617d76ebe0a2b42cdba0ebce1.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n![](images/18/1892b80eb017795a7420b59053b836bea894ff77b7478fc7f884de6b43384cb1.svg)\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." 13194139534509 17592186045417 true] [17592186153353 1064 "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." 13194139534509 17592186045417 false] [17592186247221 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x